  <section id="key-takeaways">
    <h2>Key Takeaways & Strategic Implications</h2>
    <ul class="takeaway-list">
      <li>
        <span class="priority">Temporal skew is structural, not cyclical.</span>
        80.3% of indexed abstracts originate from 2024, reflecting PubMed's inherent indexing lag. Policy analysts should treat 2025–2026 volumes as incomplete snapshots and avoid direct year-on-year count comparisons without normalization.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="priority">Open access momentum accelerated in 2025.</span>
        The OA rate rose from 64.1% in 2024 to 74.1% in 2025 — a +10.0 percentage-point jump. However, the 2026 partial-year rate (53.8%) is artificially depressed by small-sample volatility (n=225). Funders should anticipate sustained OA pressure and plan APC budgets accordingly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="priority">China dominates volume but lags on open access.</span>
        China contributes 22.9% of all abstracts yet achieves only 54.7% OA — the lowest among top-10 countries. This signals a policy gap that could affect global knowledge-equity initiatives and collaborative funding frameworks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="priority">COVID-19 research is declining; mental health and neuro topics are accelerating.</span>
        COVID-19 dropped 28.1% in normalized share from 2024 to 2025. Conversely, Stress Disorders (Post-Traumatic) and Electroencephalography showed the strongest normalized growth (+255% and +166%, respectively), suggesting a pivot toward mental-health and neuroscience funding priorities.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="priority">Collaboration intensity varies sharply by geography and topic.</span>
        Italy and China show the highest median author counts (8.0 and 7.0), while Quality-of-Life and Coleoptera studies cluster at the low end (4.0 and 3.5). Cancer and microbiome topics attract larger teams, indicating higher resource requirements.
