    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="geographic-landscape">
    <h2>Geographic Landscape of Biomedical Research</h2>
    <p>Biomedical research output in this dataset is geographically concentrated: the top 10 identifiable countries account for {top10_share:.1f}% of all abstracts. China leads decisively with {top10_real.iloc[0]:,} publications ({top10_real.iloc[0]/len(df)*100:.1f}%), followed by the USA ({top10_real.iloc[1]:,}, {top10_real.iloc[1]/len(df)*100:.1f}%) and India ({top10_real.iloc[2]:,}, {top10_real.iloc[2]/len(df)*100:.1f}%).</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['top_countries']}" alt="Top contributing countries">
    </div>

    <p>The relative country rankings remain stable across 2024–2026, with China, USA, and India holding the top three positions in every year. However, the <em>proportional</em> contributions shift slightly: China's share of the known 2026 cohort is 19.6% (44/225), down from 22.9% overall, while the USA holds steady at ~11%. These shifts are within the margin of error given small 2026 sample sizes.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['country_shift']}" alt="Country volume over time">
    </div>

    <h3>Collaboration Intensity by Geography</h3>
    <p>Median author count varies meaningfully by country. Italy leads with 8.0 median authors per paper, followed by China (7.0), Brazil (7.0), and Switzerland (7.0). The USA sits at 6.0, aligning with the global median. Higher author counts in Italy and China may reflect larger clinical consortia, multi-site trials, or national research programs that encourage broad authorship.</p>

