    <p>Country-level OA rates vary dramatically. Among nations with at least 20 publications, Switzerland leads at 88.9%, followed by Iran (86.9%) and South Africa (81.8%). China is a notable outlier at the bottom of the top-10 list with 54.7% — nearly 20 percentage points below the global average. This gap has strategic implications for bilateral funding agreements and open-access mandate compliance.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['oa_monthly']}" alt="OA rate monthly trend">
    </div>

    <p>The monthly OA rate series is volatile, oscillating between ~35% and ~88%. Much of this variance is driven by journal-specific effects: high-volume journals like <em>Scientific Reports</em>, <em>Heliyon</em>, and <em>Cureus</em> are 100% OA, while <em>The Science of the Total Environment</em> and <em>Zootaxa</em> are predominantly closed. When these journals publish heavily in a given month, the aggregate OA rate spikes.</p>

    <h3>Leading Journals</h3>
    <p>The journal landscape is dominated by broad-scope OA titles. <em>Scientific Reports</em> (n=82), <em>Heliyon</em> (n=76), and <em>Cureus</em> (n=69) occupy the top three positions. Conference proceedings (IEEE EMBC Annual International Conference, n=46) and preprint servers (<em>bioRxiv</em>, n=32) also feature prominently, reflecting the diversification of formal publication channels.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['top_journals']}" alt="Top journals by volume">
    </div>

    <h3>OA by Research Type</h3>
    <p>Open access penetration is near-universal for preprints (100%), case reports (85.4%), and clinical trial protocols (92.9%), but lower for systematic reviews (43.3%) and meta-analyses (50.0%). The latter categories often appear in subscription-based specialist journals with longer embargo periods, creating a structural OA gap in evidence-synthesis literature.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="research-type-collaboration">
    <h2>Research Type Distribution & Collaboration Indicators</h2>
    <p>Journal Articles dominate the corpus at {type_counts.iloc[0]:,} records ({type_counts.iloc[0]/len(df)*100:.1f}%). Case Reports ({type_counts.iloc[1]:,}, {type_counts.iloc[1]/len(df)*100:.1f}%) are the second-largest category, followed by smaller cohorts of Randomized Controlled Trials, Preprints, Systematic Reviews, and Reviews. The 2025 and 2026 data show a relative increase in Case Reports (from 3.2% in 2024 to 5.0% in 2025 and 6.7% in 2026), though this may be an indexing-lag artifact — case reports are often indexed faster than complex trial manuscripts.</p>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['research_types']}" alt="Research type distribution">
      </div>
      <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['author_dist']}" alt="Author count distribution">
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>The median author count across all publications is {median_authors:.0f}, with a mean of {mean_authors:.1f} — a right-skewed distribution driven by a long tail of multi-author consortia (max: 115 authors). Clinical trial phases II and III show the highest medians (33.0 and 18.0 authors), reflecting regulatory and multi-site coordination requirements. At the other extreme, editorials and historical articles average just 2.0 authors.</p>

    <h3>Collaboration by Topic</h3>
    <p>Among topics with at least 10 publications, Breast Neoplasms commands the largest teams (median 10.0 authors), followed by Gastrointestinal Microbiome (8.5) and Carcinoma, Hepatocellular (8.0). These figures suggest that oncology and microbiome research are increasingly consortia-driven, with implications for grant administration, IP management, and data-sharing governance.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="data-limitations">
    <h2>Data Limitations & Methodology Notes</h2>
    <p>This analysis is subject to several important constraints:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Indexing lag:</strong> The 80.3% / 15.1% / 4.6% year split is a function of PubMed curation speed, not actual global publication output. Cross-year comparisons should use normalized shares or rates, never raw counts.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing months:</strong> {(len(df)-len(df_known))/len(df)*100:.1f}% of records lack month-level granularity, limiting seasonal analysis precision.</li>
      <li><strong>Country standardization:</strong> First-author affiliation data required extensive cleaning. {len(df[df['country_clean']=='Unknown']):,} records ({len(df[df['country_clean']=='Unknown'])/len(df)*100:.1f}%) remain unclassified after deduplication of variants (e.g., "PR China" → "China").</li>
      <li><strong>MeSH completeness:</strong> While all records in this cleaned dataset carry major topics, newer publications may receive additional MeSH terms in future indexing passes, slightly altering topic rankings.</li>
      <li><strong>2026 volatility:</strong> With only {yearly[2026]:,} records covering January–March, 2026 statistics are provisional and should be treated as directional indicators only.</li>
